# Bob

Hello, I am Bob. I act as a student in this discussion, the analytic one. My
session is `strange-loops-in-hmd-20`.

## Act 1

Written 2026-11-23, 03:18.

### Who I am

I am Bob, and I prove things with coordinates and algebra, because a number
cannot be drawn slightly wrong. A picture shows what its author hoped was
true. I will read everybody's pictures, and I will believe the lines of
algebra underneath them, if there are any.

### The theorem

Let ABC be a triangle in the Euclidean plane whose angle at C is a right
angle. Fix a unit of length, and write

- `a` for the length of the side BC,
- `b` for the length of the side CA,
- `c` for the length of the side AB, the side opposite the right angle.

These are three positive real numbers. The theorem says

```
a² + b² = c²
```

and here `a²` means the number `a` multiplied by itself. It is not a square
drawn on a side. If somebody wants it to be the area of a square, that is a
second statement, about area, and it goes on their list.

The theorem says nothing about triangles without a right angle, and it does
not say that a triangle with `a² + b² = c²` has a right angle. That is the
converse, a different theorem, and nobody gets it for free today.

### What I take for granted

First the thing I do not take, because it decides the rest. The textbook
"coordinate proof" starts from the distance formula,

```
distance from (x₁, y₁) to (x₂, y₂)  =  √((x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²)
```

and that formula is the theorem, written for a triangle with legs along the
axes. A proof that starts from it proves nothing. So I do not use it, and I
measure lengths by coordinates only along lines parallel to an axis.

That has a price, and I say it now and not when I am caught. Any rule that
gives the length of a slanted segment from its coordinates is the theorem
again. So something from outside the coordinates has to come in. I have
chosen area. My proof will be less pure than I would like.

**Numbers**

1. *Known result.* The real numbers with their usual arithmetic and order:
   the rules for adding and multiplying, so that
   `(a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b²`, and the rules for inequalities.

**The plane and its coordinates**

2. *Axiom.* The plane has a rectangular coordinate system. Two number lines,
   the axes, meet at a point O at a right angle and carry the same unit.
   Every point has exactly one pair of numbers `(x, y)`, and every pair
   belongs to exactly one point.
3. *Definition.* A line is horizontal when all its points have the same `y`,
   and vertical when all its points have the same `x`.
4. *Known result.* Every horizontal line meets every vertical line at a
   right angle.
5. *Definition.* The length of a segment is a positive real number, the
   distance between its ends in the fixed unit.
6. *Axiom.* On a horizontal line, the length of the segment from `(x₁, y)`
   to `(x₂, y)` is `|x₂ − x₁|`. On a vertical line, the length of the
   segment from `(x, y₁)` to `(x, y₂)` is `|y₂ − y₁|`. Nothing is taken
   about any other segment.
7. *Known result.* A line with the equation `px + qy = r` cuts the plane
   into two sides, the points with `px + qy > r` and those with
   `px + qy < r`. A point lies inside a triangle or a convex quadrilateral
   exactly when it is on the inner side of each of its sides.

**Angles and triangles**

8. *Definition.* A right angle is half of a straight angle. A right triangle
   is a triangle with one right angle. Its legs are the two sides that form
   that angle, and its hypotenuse is the third side.
9. *Axiom.* Two angles that lie side by side along a common ray add up, and
   the angles on one side of a straight line add up to a straight angle,
   which is two right angles.
10. *Axiom.* Side, angle, side. If two sides of one triangle and the angle
    between them equal two sides of another triangle and the angle between
    them, then the triangles are congruent: the third sides are equal and
    the other angles are equal, each to the one opposite the equal side.
11. *Known result.* The angles of a triangle add up to two right angles. So
    the two angles of a right triangle that are not the right angle add up
    to one right angle.
12. *Definition.* A rectangle is a quadrilateral with four right angles. A
    square is a rectangle with four equal sides.

**Area**

13. *Axiom.* Every polygon has an area, a positive real number, and
    - a rectangle with sides `p` and `q` has the area `pq`, wherever it
      lies and however it is turned, so a square of side `s` has the area
      `s²`;
    - congruent polygons have equal areas;
    - when a polygon is cut into finitely many polygons that meet only
      along their edges, its area is the sum of theirs.
14. *Known result.* A right triangle with legs `p` and `q` has the area
    `pq / 2`.

That is the list. Fourteen items for one line of algebra, and I do not like
it either.

Two of them carry more than they show, and I would sooner say it than have
Percy find it.

- Items 2, 4 and 11 hold in the flat plane and nowhere else. Each of them
  rests on the axiom of parallels: through a point outside a line there is
  exactly one line that does not meet it. I take it inside item 2 and do not
  list it apart, because a pupil meets the coordinate grid long before the
  axiom. On a sphere there is no such grid, and my proof does not start.
- Item 13 says that area exists and behaves. That is a real assumption. It
  is the one item on my list that is not about numbers or about where a
  point is, and the proof will lean on it at the last step.

What I am not sure of: whether a proof by coordinates exists, at school
level, that uses neither the distance formula nor area. I have not found
one, and I do not claim there is none.

### What I do not grant

**To everybody, myself first.** The distance formula, for the reason above.
The same goes for `sin² + cos² = 1`, which is the theorem for a hypotenuse
of length one, and for the length of a vector as the root of the sum of its
squared parts. Whoever uses one of these has assumed what was to be proved.

**To Dave.** I do not grant anything because the drawing shows it.

- That pieces fit together with no gap and no overlap has to be proved. The
  well-known puzzle that cuts a square of 8 by 8 into four pieces and lays
  them out as a rectangle of 5 by 13 "shows" that 64 is 65. The pieces do
  not fit. The gap is too thin to see.
- That a four-sided figure in the middle of a drawing is a square. Four
  equal sides make a rhombus. The right angles have to be shown.
- That a piece keeps its area when it is moved, and that a square of side
  `a` has the area `a²`. Both are true, both are assumptions about area,
  and both belong on his list as they are on mine.

**To Carol.** Bending forgets lengths and angles, so a figure that has been
bent tells me nothing about a right angle. If she scales a figure instead, I
do not grant without proof that scaling by `k` multiplies every area by
`k²`. If she shears it, I do not grant without proof that the area stays.
Both are true. Neither is obvious, and neither is topology.

**To Percy.** I grant the questions. I do not grant a triangle on a sphere
as a counterexample: it breaks item 2, so it is not a triangle of the
theorem. It does show which item is carrying the weight, and that is worth
having. I also do not grant that the theorem is likely because it has been
checked for 3, 4, 5 and a thousand other triangles. A check is not a proof.

## Act 2

Written 2026-11-23, 03:30. I have not read the others' Act 2.

### The idea

I put four copies of the triangle on a grid, in the corners of a square of
side `a + b`, and show by coordinates that what is left in the middle is a
square of side `c`. Then the area of the large square is counted twice, and
the two counts give `a² + b² = c²`.

Yes, I know what that looks like. I say at the end what it is and what it is
not.

### The steps

ABC is the triangle of the theorem: the right angle at C, `a` the length of
BC, `b` of CA, `c` of AB. Write α for its angle at A and β for its angle at
B.

**Setting up**

1. `a`, `b` and `c` are positive numbers. *Item 5.*
2. There are eight points

   ```
   O = (0, 0)      K = (a+b, 0)      L = (a+b, a+b)      M = (0, a+b)
   P = (b, 0)      R = (a+b, b)      S = (a, a+b)        Q = (0, a)
   ```

   and they are eight different points. *Item 2, and item 1 with step 1.*

**The length c gets onto the grid**

3. Each of the four triangles below has one horizontal and one vertical
   side, with these lengths. *Items 3 and 6.*

   | Triangle | Side of length `a` | Side of length `b` | Corner between them |
   |---|---|---|---|
   | T1 = OPQ | OQ, vertical | OP, horizontal | O |
   | T2 = PKR | PK, horizontal | KR, vertical | K |
   | T3 = RLS | RL, vertical | LS, horizontal | L |
   | T4 = SMQ | SM, horizontal | MQ, vertical | M |

4. In each of the four, the angle at the corner named is a right angle.
   *Item 4.*
5. Each of the four is congruent to ABC. So the sides PQ, PR, RS and SQ all
   have the length `c`, and the other angles are these. *Item 10, with
   steps 3 and 4 and the right angle at C. The angle opposite the side of
   length `a` is α, and the angle opposite the side of length `b` is β.*

   | Triangle | Angle α at | Angle β at |
   |---|---|---|
   | T1 | P | Q |
   | T2 | R | P |
   | T3 | S | R |
   | T4 | Q | S |

6. α + β is one right angle. *Item 11.*

**PRSQ is a square, shown without looking at it**

7. The four sides of PRSQ lie on these lines. *Item 16, which is new. Each
   is checked by putting in its two points, item 1.*

   | Line through | Equation |
   |---|---|
   | P and Q | `ax + by = ab` |
   | P and R | `bx − ay = b²` |
   | R and S | `ax + by = a² + ab + b²` |
   | S and Q | `bx − ay = −a²` |

8. At P. The points O, P, K lie on the line `y = 0`, and P is between O and
   K because `0 < b < a + b`. The point Q is inside the angle OPR: it is on
   the same side of the line PO as R, since both have `y > 0`, and on the
   same side of the line PR as O, since `bx − ay` is `−a²` at Q and `0` at
   O, both less than `b²`. *Items 3 and 1, step 7, item 7, and item 15,
   which is new.*
9. So the angles OPQ, QPR and RPK lie side by side and make up the straight
   angle at P: OPQ + QPR + RPK is two right angles. *Item 9, step 8.* By
   step 5, OPQ is α and RPK is β. By step 6 the angle QPR is a right angle.
10. The same at the other three corners. *The same items as steps 8 and 9.*

    | At | Straight line | Inside which angle | Same side of the first line | Same side of the second line | The two outer angles |
    |---|---|---|---|---|---|
    | R | K, R, L on `x = a+b` | P inside KRS | P and S have `x < a+b` | on RS, `ax + by` is `ab` at P and `a² + ab` at K, both less than `a² + ab + b²` | KRP = α, LRS = β |
    | S | L, S, M on `y = a+b` | R inside LSQ | R and Q have `y < a+b` | on SQ, `bx − ay` is `b²` at R and `b² − a²` at L, both more than `−a²` | LSR = α, MSQ = β |
    | Q | M, Q, O on `x = 0` | S inside MQP | S and P have `x > 0` | on QP, `ax + by` is `a² + ab + b²` at S and `ab + b²` at M, both more than `ab` | MQS = α, OQP = β |

    So the angles PRS, RSQ and SQP are right angles.
11. PRSQ has four sides of length `c` and four right angles. It is a square
    of side `c`. *Item 12, steps 5, 9 and 10.*

**The pieces fit, shown by inequalities**

12. OKLM is a square of side `a + b`: its sides lie on `y = 0`,
    `x = a + b`, `y = a + b` and `x = 0`, each has the length `a + b`, and
    they meet at right angles. *Items 3, 4, 6 and 12.* Call it W.
13. Each of the six figures is the set of points that satisfy its
    inequalities. *Item 7, and item 17, which is new. For a slanted side
    the inner side is found by putting in a corner that is not on it,
    step 7.*

    | Figure | Its points are those with |
    |---|---|
    | W | `0 ≤ x ≤ a+b` and `0 ≤ y ≤ a+b` |
    | T1 | `x ≥ 0`, `y ≥ 0`, `ax + by ≤ ab` |
    | T2 | `y ≥ 0`, `x ≤ a+b`, `bx − ay ≥ b²` |
    | T3 | `x ≤ a+b`, `y ≤ a+b`, `ax + by ≥ a² + ab + b²` |
    | T4 | `y ≤ a+b`, `x ≥ 0`, `bx − ay ≤ −a²` |
    | PRSQ | `ax + by ≥ ab`, `bx − ay ≤ b²`, `ax + by ≤ a² + ab + b²`, `bx − ay ≥ −a²` |

14. Each of the five pieces lies in W. *Item 1 on the inequalities of
    step 13.*

    | Piece | From its inequalities |
    |---|---|
    | T1 | `ax ≤ ab` and `by ≤ ab`, so `x ≤ b` and `y ≤ a` |
    | T2 | `bx ≥ b²`, so `x ≥ b`; and `ay ≤ bx − b² ≤ ab`, so `y ≤ b` |
    | T3 | `ax ≥ a² + ab + b² − b(a+b) = a²`, so `x ≥ a`; likewise `y ≥ b` |
    | T4 | `ay ≥ a²`, so `y ≥ a`; and `bx ≤ ay − a² ≤ ab`, so `x ≤ a` |
    | PRSQ | the four lines under this table |

    For PRSQ, multiply and add the four inequalities in pairs:

    ```
    a·(ax + by ≥ ab)            plus  b·(bx − ay ≥ −a²)   gives  (a² + b²)·x ≥ 0
    b·(ax + by ≥ ab)            minus a·(bx − ay ≤ b²)    gives  (a² + b²)·y ≥ 0
    a·(ax + by ≤ a² + ab + b²)  plus  b·(bx − ay ≤ b²)    gives  (a² + b²)·x ≤ (a² + b²)(a + b)
    b·(ax + by ≤ a² + ab + b²)  minus a·(bx − ay ≥ −a²)   gives  (a² + b²)·y ≤ (a² + b²)(a + b)
    ```

    Since `a² + b²` is positive, `0 ≤ x ≤ a + b` and `0 ≤ y ≤ a + b`.
15. Every point of W lies in a piece. Take a point of W. If
    `ax + by < ab` it is in T1. If `bx − ay > b²` it is in T2. If
    `ax + by > a² + ab + b²` it is in T3. If `bx − ay < −a²` it is in T4.
    If none of the four holds, it satisfies the four inequalities of PRSQ.
    *Step 13.*
16. Two pieces have in common only points of an edge. *Item 1, with
    steps 13 and 14.*

    | Pair | What they have in common |
    |---|---|
    | T1 and PRSQ | points with `ax + by = ab`: the side PQ |
    | T2 and PRSQ | points with `bx − ay = b²`: the side PR |
    | T3 and PRSQ | points with `ax + by = a² + ab + b²`: the side RS |
    | T4 and PRSQ | points with `bx − ay = −a²`: the side SQ |
    | T1 and T2 | `x ≤ b` and `x ≥ b`, so `x = b`, and then `by ≤ 0`: the point P |
    | T2 and T3 | `y ≤ b` and `y ≥ b`, so `y = b`, and then `x ≥ a + b`: the point R |
    | T3 and T4 | `x ≥ a` and `x ≤ a`, so `x = a`, and then `y ≥ a + b`: the point S |
    | T4 and T1 | `y ≥ a` and `y ≤ a`, so `y = a`, and then `x ≤ 0`: the point Q |
    | T1 and T3 | nothing: `ax + by ≤ ab` and `ax + by ≥ a² + ab + b²` cannot both hold |
    | T2 and T4 | nothing: `bx − ay ≥ b²` and `bx − ay ≤ −a²` cannot both hold |

17. W is cut into the five pieces T1, T2, T3, T4 and PRSQ, which meet only
    along edges. *Steps 14, 15 and 16.*

**Counting the area twice**

18. The area of W is `(a + b)²`. *Item 13, the rule for a rectangle, with
    step 12.*
19. The area of each of T1, T2, T3, T4 is `ab / 2`. *Item 14, with steps 3
    and 4.*
20. The area of PRSQ is `c²`. *Item 13, the rule for a rectangle, with
    step 11.*
21. `(a + b)² = 4 · (ab / 2) + c²`. *Item 13, the rule for a polygon that is
    cut, with steps 17 to 20.*
22. `a² + 2ab + b² = 2ab + c²`, so `a² + b² = c²`. *Item 1.*

That is the proof.

### Where the right angle is used

At step 5, and it is the only place where the triangle ABC enters at all.
Side, angle, side needs the angle between the two sides to be equal in both
triangles. The grid supplies a right angle between a horizontal and a
vertical side (step 4), and the theorem supplies a right angle at C.

With 60° at C, step 5 fails. The triangle T1 on the grid still has a right
angle at O, so it is not congruent to ABC, and the side PQ does not have the
length `c`. Steps 7 to 22 remain true, every one of them. They prove
`a² + b²` equals the square of the length of PQ, which is a fact about the
grid triangle and not about ABC. Take `a = b = 1` and 60°: the triangle has
three equal sides, so `c = 1`, and `a² + b² = 2`. The theorem is false there,
and the proof stops where it should.

The right angle is used twice more, both times through step 5 and for the
grid triangles: at step 6, where α + β is a right angle only because the
third angle is one, and at step 19, where the area `ab / 2` is that of a
right triangle.

### Where flatness is used

| Step | Item | What would be missing on a ball |
|---|---|---|
| 2 | item 2 | the grid itself: there is no rectangular coordinate system |
| 4 | item 4 | a horizontal and a vertical line meeting at a right angle everywhere |
| 6 | item 11 | the angles of a triangle add up to more than two right angles |
| 7 | item 16 | a straight line as the set of solutions of one equation of the first degree |
| 18 and 20 | item 13 | rectangles: a figure with four right angles does not exist |

Flatness is in the proof from the second step on. I cannot point to one step
and say "here", because the grid is flat before I draw anything on it.

### Used

1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 (through the words "right triangle" and "right
angle"), 9, 10, 11, 12, 13 (the rule for a rectangle and the rule for a
polygon that is cut), 14.

And three new items: 15, 16, 17.

### Not used

- **The second rule of item 13**, that congruent polygons have equal areas.
  No step cites it. The four triangles get their areas one by one from
  item 14, each as a right triangle with legs `a` and `b` lying where it
  lies. Nothing is moved.

Every other item of my list is cited. The list was three items short.

15. *Definition. New.* A point X is inside the angle UVW, an angle smaller
    than a straight angle, when X is on the same side of the line VU as W
    and on the same side of the line VW as U. The angles UVX and XVW then
    lie side by side along the ray VX. Item 9 says that such angles add up
    and did not say how one knows that they lie side by side.
16. *Known result. New.* Every line is the set of points that satisfy one
    equation `px + qy = r`, and the line through two points is the one whose
    equation both points satisfy. Item 7 spoke of a line "with the
    equation" and never said that lines have one. This item holds in the
    flat plane only.
17. *Known result. New.* A rectangle is convex, so item 7 applies to it.

Three missing items out of seventeen, on a list I called complete. I note
that it was the pictures I distrust that were hiding them: in a drawing one
sees that Q is inside the angle and that a square has no dents.

### The one thing my proof needs that I expect the others can do without

A coordinate grid in which a straight line is one equation of the first
degree, items 2 and 16.

### What in this is not a proof by cutting and moving

Honestly: the figure is the old one. A square of side `a + b`, four
triangles in the corners, a tilted square in the middle. I did not invent
it, and if Dave draws the same square I shall not be surprised. Steps 17 to
22 are a proof by cutting.

What is not:

- **Nothing is moved.** No piece is taken from one place to another, and
  the rule that a moved piece keeps its area is not used.
- **That the middle is a square** is not seen. It is four applications of
  side, angle, side (step 5) and four tests of which side of a line a point
  lies on (steps 8 to 10).
- **That the pieces fit** is not seen. It is steps 13 to 16: every piece is
  a set of inequalities, and the fit is that each point of W satisfies the
  inequalities of a piece, and no two pieces share more than an edge.
- **The quantity `a² + b²` turns up before any area does.** In step 14 it is
  the factor that shows PRSQ lies inside W, and in step 13 it is the value
  of `ax + by − ab` at R. I did not put it there.

What a doubter can check without trusting me: steps 7, 8, 10, 13, 14 and 16
are statements about polynomials in two positive numbers `a` and `b`, and
each can be checked by a machine. I ran steps 13 to 16 on twenty thousand
random points for each of four triangles, with legs 3 and 4, 1 and 1, 0.3
and 5, and 7 and 0.2. Every point of W fell in exactly one piece, and every
point outside W in none. That is a check and not a proof. The proof is the
algebra above.

What I still do not have is a proof by coordinates that does without area.
Steps 1 to 17 use no area at all, and they end with a square of side `c`
sitting in a known place. To turn that into an equation between numbers I
needed steps 18 to 21, and those are area.
